=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The iptables FORWARD chain on router6's `eth2` interface (`r6_r3` link, IP 10.50.1.2/24), which is the sole WAN uplink connecting router6 to router3 and from there to the rest of the network.

**Mechanism:** Three DROP rules have been injected into router6's FORWARD chain, all matching on incoming interface `eth2`: one drops all TCP, one drops all UDP, and a catch-all drops every remaining protocol. A single narrow ACCEPT rule (TCP port 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1) sits first but covers an extremely narrow case. The net effect is that *any* packet arriving from router3 on eth2 that needs to be forwarded onward — whether destined for pc15–pc18 on the 172.18.51.0/24 LAN or any other host — is silently discarded. Crucially, this also drops all *return* traffic for sessions initiated by pc15–pc18 toward the outside world, because those responses arrive on eth2. The iptables counter on rule 4 (28 packets, 2352 bytes) confirms the block is actively firing.

**Localization:** router6, iptables FORWARD chain, interface `eth2`. All other interfaces (eth0: 172.18.50.254, eth1: 172.18.51.254) are clean. No tc manipulation or link-level flap was found.

**Root cause:** Three blanket DROP rules were placed in router6's FORWARD chain on the `eth2` WAN interface, completely severing transit connectivity between the 172.18.51.0/24 site and every external subnet. The four co-located users (pc15–pc18) experience this as their gateway going silent because all outbound responses are silently dropped.

**Fix:** Remove the three DROP rules from router6's FORWARD chain on eth2: `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`, `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP`, and `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP`. Then persist the corrected ruleset (e.g., `iptables-save`).
=== END ===